 Chapter 8 Beta diversity Between-sample dissimilarity using Hill-based distances (richness, neutral, phylogenetic, functional), PERMANOVA tests for broad environment/island effects, and NMDS ordinations to visualise separation in community structure. Note: Load all required data objects including genome counts, metadata, and color schemes. load(&quot;data/data.Rdata&quot;) treatment_colors &lt;- c(&quot;#f56042&quot;,&quot;#429ef5&quot;, &quot;#42f58d&quot;, &quot;#b142f5&quot;, &quot;#f5e642&quot;) Note: Filter out sample EHI01340, identify valid samples, remove samples with missing broad_environment values, and filter genome counts to only include valid samples. sample_metadata &lt;- sample_metadata %&gt;% dplyr::filter(sample != &quot;EHI01340&quot;) valid_samples &lt;- sample_metadata$sample sample_metadata_without_NA &lt;- sample_metadata %&gt;% dplyr::filter(!is.na(broad_environment)) genome_counts_filt &lt;- genome_counts_filt %&gt;% dplyr::select(genome, tidyselect::any_of(valid_samples)) beta_div Note: Calculate four types of beta diversity distance matrices using Hill-based pairwise distances: richness-based (q=0, presence/absence), neutral/abundance-weighted (q=1), phylogenetic (q=1 with phylogenetic tree), and functional (q=1 with functional trait distances). Filters out genomes and samples with zero counts before calculation. restore_beta_sample_labels &lt;- function(beta_obj, sample_ids) { beta_mat &lt;- as.matrix(beta_obj$S) rownames(beta_mat) &lt;- colnames(beta_mat) &lt;- sample_ids beta_obj$S &lt;- stats::as.dist(beta_mat) beta_obj } # Prefer cached matrices for webbook renders (hillpair is slow). if (file.exists(&quot;data/beta.Rdata&quot;)) { load(&quot;data/beta.Rdata&quot;) message(&quot;Loaded beta diversity matrices from data/beta.Rdata&quot;) } else { counts_for_beta &lt;- genome_counts_filt %&gt;% tibble::column_to_rownames(&quot;genome&quot;) %&gt;% dplyr::filter(rowSums(. != 0, na.rm = TRUE) &gt; 0) %&gt;% dplyr::select_if(~!all(. == 0)) sample_ids &lt;- colnames(counts_for_beta) # Presence/Absence beta_q0n &lt;- counts_for_beta %&gt;% hillpair(., q = 0) %&gt;% restore_beta_sample_labels(., sample_ids) # Abundance-sensitive beta_q1n &lt;- counts_for_beta %&gt;% hillpair(., q = 1) %&gt;% restore_beta_sample_labels(., sample_ids) # Abundance + Phylogeny genome_counts_filt_beta &lt;- counts_for_beta %&gt;% tibble::rownames_to_column(&quot;genome&quot;) genome_tree_beta &lt;- keep.tip(genome_tree, tip = genome_counts_filt_beta$genome) beta_q1p &lt;- counts_for_beta %&gt;% hillpair(., q = 1, tree = genome_tree_beta) %&gt;% restore_beta_sample_labels(., sample_ids) } Note: Calculate functional beta diversity (beta_q1f) by matching genomes between count data and functional trait data (genome_gifts). Converts GIFT traits to elements, calculates Gower distance matrix, filters and aligns both datasets to common genomes, then computes Hill-based pairwise distances with functional trait distances. # Create genome_counts_filt_beta if it doesn&#39;t exist (from beta_div chunk) if (!exists(&quot;genome_counts_filt_beta&quot;)) { genome_counts_filt_beta &lt;- genome_counts_filt %&gt;% tibble::column_to_rownames(., &quot;genome&quot;) %&gt;% dplyr::filter(rowSums(. != 0, na.rm = TRUE) &gt; 0) %&gt;% dplyr::select_if(~!all(. == 0)) %&gt;% tibble::rownames_to_column(., &quot;genome&quot;) } # 1. Only use genomes that also occur in genome_gifts valid_genomes &lt;- genome_counts_filt_beta$genome[genome_counts_filt_beta$genome %in% rownames(genome_gifts)] # 2. filter genome_gifts on these genomes genome_gifts1 &lt;- genome_gifts[valid_genomes, ] genome_gifts1 &lt;- genome_gifts1[, colSums(genome_gifts1 != 0) &gt; 0] # 3. Calculate functional distance matrix dist &lt;- genome_gifts1 %&gt;% to.elements(., GIFT_db) %&gt;% traits2dist(., method = &quot;gower&quot;) # 4. Determine genomes that exist in both genome_counts_filt_beta and dist common_genomes &lt;- intersect( genome_counts_filt_beta$genome, rownames(dist) ) # 5. Align count matrix rows to dist matrix order (common_genomes) genome_counts_filt_matched &lt;- genome_counts_filt %&gt;% dplyr::filter(genome %in% common_genomes) %&gt;% dplyr::mutate(genome = factor(genome, levels = common_genomes)) %&gt;% dplyr::arrange(genome) %&gt;% tibble::column_to_rownames(&quot;genome&quot;) %&gt;% dplyr::select_if(~!all(. == 0)) # 6. Limit and sort dist matrix to common genomes dist_matched &lt;- dist[common_genomes, common_genomes] beta_q1f &lt;- genome_counts_filt_matched %&gt;% hillpair(q = 1, dist = dist_matched) beta_q1f &lt;- restore_beta_sample_labels(beta_q1f, colnames(genome_counts_filt_matched)) Note: Save all four beta diversity distance matrices (richness, neutral, phylogenetic, functional) to an R data file for later use. Note: Set random seed for reproducibility of permutation-based statistical tests. 8.1 Permanova Note: Define a reusable function run_permanova that performs PERMANOVA analysis on beta diversity matrices. The function matches samples between distance matrix and metadata, filters out NAs, tests beta dispersion (homogeneity of variances), runs PERMANOVA with adonis2, optionally performs pairwise comparisons, and returns both dispersion and PERMANOVA results. run_permanova &lt;- function(beta_matrix, metadata, sample_col = &quot;sample&quot;, group_col, group_vars, pairwise = FALSE) { # 1. Sample labels from Beta-Matrix (dist object) sample_labels &lt;- labels(beta_matrix$S) # 2. Metadata filtered and sorted metadata_matched &lt;- metadata %&gt;% dplyr::filter(!!rlang::sym(sample_col) %in% sample_labels) %&gt;% dplyr::arrange(match(!!rlang::sym(sample_col), sample_labels)) # 3. Synchronize beta_matrix$S to metadata (subset via matrix, then back to dist) common_samples &lt;- intersect(sample_labels, metadata_matched[[sample_col]]) S_mat &lt;- as.matrix(beta_matrix$S) S_mat &lt;- S_mat[common_samples, common_samples, drop = FALSE] beta_matrix$S &lt;- as.dist(S_mat) metadata_matched &lt;- metadata_matched %&gt;% dplyr::filter(!!rlang::sym(sample_col) %in% common_samples) %&gt;% dplyr::arrange(match(!!rlang::sym(sample_col), common_samples)) # 4. Remove samples with NA in group_col (again via matrix → dist) valid_idx &lt;- !is.na(metadata_matched[[group_col]]) S_mat &lt;- as.matrix(beta_matrix$S) S_mat &lt;- S_mat[valid_idx, valid_idx, drop = FALSE] beta_matrix$S &lt;- as.dist(S_mat) metadata_matched &lt;- metadata_matched[valid_idx, ] n_groups &lt;- length(unique(metadata_matched[[group_col]])) if (n_groups &lt; 2L) { warning( &quot;Skipping PERMANOVA: &#39;&quot;, group_col, &quot;&#39; has &quot;, n_groups, &quot; level(s) after matching (&quot;, nrow(metadata_matched), &quot; samples).&quot; ) return(list(dispersion = NULL, permanova = NULL)) } # 5. Betadisper and Permutest cat(&quot;
===== Beta Dispersion Test =====
&quot;) dispersion_result &lt;- betadisper(beta_matrix$S, metadata_matched[[group_col]]) print(permutest(dispersion_result)) # 6. Dynamic formula building formula_str &lt;- paste0(&quot;beta_matrix$S ~ &quot;, paste(group_vars, collapse = &quot; + &quot;)) cat(&quot;
===== PERMANOVA Test (adonis2) =====
&quot;) permanova_result &lt;- adonis2( as.formula(formula_str), data = metadata_matched, permutations = 999, by = &quot;terms&quot; ) print(broom::tidy(permanova_result)) # 7. (Optional) Pairwise Adonis if (pairwise) { cat(&quot;
===== Pairwise PERMANOVA =====
&quot;) print(pairwise.adonis(beta_matrix$S, metadata_matched[[group_col]], perm = 999)) } # 8. Return outputs return(list( dispersion = dispersion_result, permanova = permanova_result )) } 8.1.1 Richness Note: Run PERMANOVA analysis on richness-based beta diversity (beta_q0n) to test for differences in community composition across broad environment types and islands, including interaction effects. Tests whether presence/absence community structure differs between environments. run_permanova( beta_matrix = beta_q0n, metadata = sample_metadata_without_NA, sample_col = &quot;sample&quot;, group_col = &quot;broad_environment&quot;, group_vars = c(&quot;broad_environment&quot;, &quot;island&quot;, &quot;broad_environment:island&quot;), pairwise = FALSE ) 8.1.2 Neutral diversity Note: Run PERMANOVA analysis on abundance-weighted beta diversity (beta_q1n) to test for differences in community composition across broad environment types and islands, including interaction effects and pairwise comparisons. Tests whether abundance-weighted community structure differs between environments. run_permanova( beta_matrix = beta_q1n, metadata = sample_metadata_without_NA, sample_col = &quot;sample&quot;, group_col = &quot;broad_environment&quot;, group_vars = c(&quot;broad_environment&quot;, &quot;island&quot;, &quot;broad_environment:island&quot;), pairwise = TRUE ) 8.1.3 Phylogenetic diversity (beta_q1p) Note: Run PERMANOVA analysis on phylogenetic beta diversity (beta_q1p) to test for differences in community composition across broad environment types and islands, including interaction effects. Tests whether phylogenetically-weighted community structure differs between environments. run_permanova( beta_matrix = beta_q1p, metadata = sample_metadata_without_NA, sample_col = &quot;sample&quot;, group_col = &quot;broad_environment&quot;, group_vars = c(&quot;broad_environment&quot;, &quot;island&quot;, &quot;broad_environment:island&quot;), pairwise = FALSE # no pairwise comparisons here ) 8.1.4 Functional diversity (beta_q1f) Note: Run PERMANOVA analysis on functional beta diversity (beta_q1f) to test for differences in community composition across broad environment types and islands, including interaction effects and pairwise comparisons. Tests whether functionally-weighted community structure differs between environments. run_permanova( beta_matrix = beta_q1f, metadata = sample_metadata_without_NA, sample_col = &quot;sample&quot;, group_col = &quot;broad_environment&quot;, group_vars = c(&quot;broad_environment&quot;, &quot;island&quot;, &quot;broad_environment:island&quot;), pairwise = TRUE ) 8.2 NMDS NMDS of β-diversity (richness/neutral/phylogenetic/functional). Points are samples, coloured by broad environment and shaped by island; segments draw each point to its group centroid. Note: Define a reusable function to create NMDS ordination plots. The function performs metaMDS on the distance matrix, extracts NMDS scores, joins with sample metadata, calculates group centroids, and visualizes samples with optional labels. # Function to fit NMDS once per beta matrix (reused for limits and plotting) fit_nmds_ordination &lt;- function(beta_matrix, seed = 2025L) { set.seed(seed) nmds_obj &lt;- beta_matrix$S %&gt;% vegan::metaMDS(trymax = 500, k = 2, trace = 0) list(nmds_obj = nmds_obj, stress = nmds_obj$stress) } # Function to calculate overall axis limits from all beta matrices calculate_nmds_limits &lt;- function(beta_matrices, seed = 2025L) { nmds_fits &lt;- purrr::imap(beta_matrices, function(beta_mat, name) { fit &lt;- fit_nmds_ordination(beta_mat, seed = seed) fit$name &lt;- name fit }) names(nmds_fits) &lt;- names(beta_matrices) all_scores &lt;- purrr::map_dfr(nmds_fits, function(fit) { fit$nmds_obj %&gt;% vegan::scores() %&gt;% tibble::as_tibble(rownames = &quot;sample&quot;) }) x_range &lt;- range(all_scores$NMDS1, na.rm = TRUE) y_range &lt;- range(all_scores$NMDS2, na.rm = TRUE) # Add some padding (5% on each side) x_padding &lt;- diff(x_range) * 0.05 y_padding &lt;- diff(y_range) * 0.05 list( xlim = c(x_range[1] - x_padding, x_range[2] + x_padding), ylim = c(y_range[1] - y_padding, y_range[2] + y_padding), fits = nmds_fits ) } create_nmds_plot &lt;- function(beta_matrix, show_labels = FALSE, point_size = 3, segment_alpha = 0.3, show_ellipses = FALSE, show_centroids = TRUE, xlim = NULL, ylim = NULL, nmds_obj = NULL) { env_nmds_labels &lt;- c( &quot;1000198 - Mixed forest&quot; = &quot;Mixed forest (MF)&quot;, &quot;1000221 - Temperate woodland&quot; = &quot;Temperate woodland (TW)&quot;, &quot;1000218 - Xeric shrubland&quot; = &quot;Xeric shrubland (XS)&quot;, &quot;1000215 - Temperate shrubland&quot; = &quot;Temperate shrubland (TS)&quot;, &quot;1000245 - Cropland&quot; = &quot;Cropland (C)&quot; ) env_nmds_levels &lt;- names(env_nmds_labels) if (is.null(nmds_obj)) { nmds_obj &lt;- fit_nmds_ordination(beta_matrix)$nmds_obj } stress_value &lt;- nmds_obj$stress # Extract scores and join with metadata nmds_scores &lt;- nmds_obj %&gt;% vegan::scores() %&gt;% tibble::as_tibble(rownames = &quot;sample&quot;) %&gt;% dplyr::inner_join(sample_metadata, by = join_by(sample == sample)) %&gt;% group_by(broad_environment) %&gt;% mutate( x_cen = mean(NMDS1, na.rm = TRUE), y_cen = mean(NMDS2, na.rm = TRUE) ) %&gt;% ungroup() # Create base plot p &lt;- nmds_scores %&gt;% ggplot(aes(x = NMDS1, y = NMDS2, color = broad_environment, shape = as.factor(island))) + # Add segments from centroids to points (lighter and thinner) geom_segment( aes(x = x_cen, y = y_cen, xend = NMDS1, yend = NMDS2), alpha = segment_alpha, linewidth = 0.3, color = &quot;gray60&quot;, show.legend = FALSE ) + # Add sample points geom_point(size = point_size, alpha = 0.8) + # Color scale by broad environment scale_color_manual( values = treatment_colors, labels = env_nmds_labels, breaks = env_nmds_levels, name = &quot;Environment&quot; ) + # Shape scale scale_shape_discrete(name = &quot;Island&quot;) # Add confidence ellipses if requested if (show_ellipses) { p &lt;- p + stat_ellipse( aes(color = broad_environment), type = &quot;norm&quot;, level = 0.95, linetype = 2, alpha = 0.5, linewidth = 0.5, show.legend = FALSE ) } # Add centroid markers if requested if (show_centroids) { p &lt;- p + geom_point( data = nmds_scores %&gt;% dplyr::distinct(broad_environment, x_cen, y_cen), aes(x = x_cen, y = y_cen, color = broad_environment), shape = 21, size = 6, stroke = 2, fill = &quot;white&quot;, show.legend = FALSE ) } # Add theme and labels p &lt;- p + # Theme theme( axis.title = element_text(size = 12, face = &quot;bold&quot;), axis.text = element_text(size = 10), panel.background = element_blank(), panel.grid.major = element_line(color = &quot;gray90&quot;, linewidth = 0.3), panel.grid.minor = element_blank(), axis.line = element_line(size = 0.5, linetype = &quot;solid&quot;, colour = &quot;black&quot;), legend.text = element_text(size = 10), legend.title = element_text(size = 11, face = &quot;bold&quot;), legend.position = &quot;right&quot;, legend.box = &quot;vertical&quot;, legend.spacing = unit(0.5, &quot;cm&quot;), legend.key.size = unit(1, &quot;lines&quot;) ) + # Labels with stress value in title labs( title = paste0(&quot;NMDS (Stress = &quot;, round(stress_value, 3), &quot;)&quot;), x = &quot;NMDS1&quot;, y = &quot;NMDS2&quot;, color = &quot;Environmental context&quot;, shape = &quot;Island&quot; ) + # Legend guides guides( color = guide_legend(order = 1, override.aes = list(size = 3)), shape = guide_legend(order = 2, override.aes = list(size = 3)) ) # Add coordinate system with fixed limits if provided if (!is.null(xlim) &amp;&amp; !is.null(ylim)) { p &lt;- p + coord_fixed(ratio = 1, xlim = xlim, ylim = ylim, expand = TRUE) } else { p &lt;- p + coord_fixed(ratio = 1, expand = TRUE) } # Text labels removed - no longer adding sample IDs return(p) } Note: Calculate overall axis limits from all beta diversity matrices to ensure consistent scaling across all NMDS plots. # Calculate limits once from all beta matrices (single NMDS fit per matrix) nmds_result &lt;- calculate_nmds_limits(list( beta_q0n = beta_q0n, beta_q1n = beta_q1n, beta_q1p = beta_q1p, beta_q1f = beta_q1f )) nmds_limits &lt;- nmds_result[c(&quot;xlim&quot;, &quot;ylim&quot;)] nmds_fits &lt;- nmds_result$fits ###Richness Note: Create NMDS ordination plot for richness-based beta diversity. Performs metaMDS on the distance matrix, extracts NMDS scores, joins with sample metadata, calculates group centroids for each broad environment, and visualizes samples colored by environment and shaped by island, with segments connecting samples to their group centroids. create_nmds_plot(beta_q0n, show_labels = FALSE, xlim = nmds_limits$xlim, ylim = nmds_limits$ylim, nmds_obj = nmds_fits$beta_q0n$nmds_obj) 8.2.1 Neutral diversity Note: Create NMDS ordination plot for abundance-weighted beta diversity. Performs metaMDS on the distance matrix, extracts NMDS scores, joins with sample metadata, calculates group centroids for each broad environment, and visualizes samples colored by environment and shaped by island, with segments connecting samples to their group centroids. create_nmds_plot(beta_q1n, show_labels = FALSE, xlim = nmds_limits$xlim, ylim = nmds_limits$ylim, nmds_obj = nmds_fits$beta_q1n$nmds_obj) 8.2.2 Phylogenetic diversity Note: Create NMDS ordination plot for phylogenetic beta diversity. Performs metaMDS on the distance matrix, extracts NMDS scores, joins with sample metadata, calculates group centroids for each broad environment, and visualizes samples colored by environment and shaped by island, with segments connecting samples to their group centroids. create_nmds_plot(beta_q1p, show_labels = FALSE, xlim = nmds_limits$xlim, ylim = nmds_limits$ylim, nmds_obj = nmds_fits$beta_q1p$nmds_obj) 8.2.3 Functional diversity Note: Create NMDS ordination plot for functional beta diversity. Performs metaMDS on the distance matrix, extracts NMDS scores, joins with sample metadata, calculates group centroids for each broad environment, and visualizes samples colored by environment and shaped by island, with segments connecting samples to their group centroids. create_nmds_plot(beta_q1f, show_labels = FALSE, xlim = nmds_limits$xlim, ylim = nmds_limits$ylim, nmds_obj = nmds_fits$beta_q1f$nmds_obj) Note: Create a zoomed-in version of the functional diversity NMDS plot with axes fitted to the data range for better visualization of the functional diversity patterns. The y-axis is stretched with additional padding for improved visibility. # Calculate functional diversity specific limits with more y-axis padding func_nmds_obj &lt;- nmds_fits$beta_q1f$nmds_obj func_scores &lt;- func_nmds_obj %&gt;% vegan::scores() %&gt;% tibble::as_tibble(rownames = &quot;sample&quot;) x_range_func &lt;- range(func_scores$NMDS1, na.rm = TRUE) y_range_func &lt;- range(func_scores$NMDS2, na.rm = TRUE) # Add padding: 5% for x-axis, 15% for y-axis (more stretch on y) x_padding_func &lt;- diff(x_range_func) * 0.05 y_padding_func &lt;- diff(y_range_func) * 0.50 func_limits &lt;- list( xlim = c(x_range_func[1] - x_padding_func, x_range_func[2] + x_padding_func), ylim = c(y_range_func[1] - y_padding_func, y_range_func[2] + y_padding_func) ) create_nmds_plot(beta_q1f, show_labels = FALSE, xlim = func_limits$xlim, ylim = func_limits$ylim) Note: Save precomputed objects for chapter 15 (sample set, alpha diversity, NMDS limits). Requires landcover_wide.csv from chapter 5; skipped if land cover is not yet available.
